Third-party components
======================

linphone-sdk  (Belledonne Communications SARL, https://linphone.org)
--------------------------------------------------------------------
FSVoip links the Linphone SDK through Belledonne's official Swift Package:

    https://gitlab.linphone.org/BC/public/linphone-sdk-swift-ios
    pinned to tag 5.5.29-novideo (audio-only variant, released 2026-10-01)

Licence, as read when this version was pinned (2026-10-08):

  * The package repository's LICENSE.txt at that tag is the GNU Affero General Public License, version 3.
  * The public headers of liblinphone in the binary framework (for example core.h) carry the header
    "either version 3 of the License, or (at your option) any later version", i.e. AGPL-3.0-or-later.

FSVoip is therefore distributed under the AGPL-3.0-or-later as well. FSVoip does not modify the SDK. The complete
corresponding source of FSVoip is at https://github.com/fullstackstudio-org/fsvoip.
Belledonne also sells a commercial licence for the SDK; FSVoip does not use it.

The Swift Package ships these components (all part of the same SDK release, see the SDK's own notices for the
details and for the licences of each):

    liblinphone, mediastreamer2, belle-sip, ortp, bctoolbox, belr, belcard, lime,
    mbedTLS (mbedcrypto, mbedtls, mbedx509), and the media plugins msamr, mscodec2, msopenh264, mssilk.

Things to review before the first App Store release:
  - msamr (AMR), mssilk (SILK), mscodec2 (Codec2) and msopenh264 (Cisco's binary OpenH264 module) bring codecs that
    may carry their own licence or patent terms. FSVoip only enables Opus, G.722, PCMA and PCMU (see
    LinphoneEngine); the final set of linked frameworks is reviewed when the app is prepared for release.
  - The distribution of (A)GPL software through the Apple App Store is a known point of discussion (see
    docs/licensing.md). The fallback is a BSD-licensed engine behind the same `SipEngine` protocol.


Build and test tooling (not part of the app)
--------------------------------------------
  - XcodeGen (MIT) generates the Xcode project from ios/project.yml; it is not bundled.
  - scripts/validate-contract.ts uses ajv (MIT), ajv-formats (MIT) and yaml (ISC) to validate the API
    fixtures; they are development dependencies only.
